p=era('meiji','Meiji','\u660e\u6cbb','1868-1912','A new state enters your home','Schools, taxes, conscription, industry, constitutions, and empire make transformation personal.','A centralized state and an expanding empire','School textbook',['oath','meijiconst','iwakura','silk','ryukyu'],'A new ruling elite centralized institutions under imperial sovereignty; elected politics developed within limits.','National schooling, military service, money taxes, and industrial work altered household obligations.','Standardization expanded alongside translation; language policy also marginalized regional and colonized voices.')
m(p,'Restoration is a reconstruction','World','A government invokes the emperor while dismantling older institutions.',
'The Meiji Restoration of 1868 became the opening of a far-reaching transformation. Domains were abolished in favor of prefectural administration in 1871. Former samurai leaders helped construct institutions that undermined hereditary warrior privileges.||The Charter Oath set out broad commitments, including seeking knowledge internationally. The Iwakura Mission studied foreign institutions and attempted treaty revision. Leaders borrowed selectively, aiming to strengthen the state rather than simply become a copy of one foreign country.||National integration and imperial expansion must be studied together. Japan acquired Taiwan in 1895 and annexed Korea in 1910. The opportunities created for some people rested alongside coercion, dispossession, and unequal rule experienced by others.',
'Why is "restoration" not a complete description of Meiji change?', ['Invoking imperial legitimacy accompanied major new institutions and social transformations.','The government restored every old domain unchanged.','Foreign models were irrelevant to the new leadership.'],'The language of restoration can authorize radical institutional innovation.','ishin','\u7dad\u65b0','Renewal or restoration; associated with the political transformation beginning in 1868.')
m(p,'A constitution, but whose sovereignty?','Power','You examine the rules of the new national political system.',
'The Meiji Constitution was promulgated in 1889. It established a Diet while locating sovereignty in the emperor. Ministers and military institutions had relationships to imperial authority that differed from a system in which a cabinet depends entirely on parliamentary confidence.||The elected lower house initially represented a limited male electorate defined by tax qualifications. Popular-rights movements helped make representation an issue, but constitutional government did not mean universal participation or rights without statutory limits.||The new fiscal order included a land tax paid in money, linking state revenue and household exposure to markets. Rights, duties, and resources were being reconfigured together. Read institutional design alongside the material obligations that made state power effective.',
'What is the key difference from the postwar constitutional order?', ['The Meiji Constitution located sovereignty in the emperor rather than the people.','The Meiji system had no written constitution.','Every adult resident voted in national elections from 1889.'],'A parliament alone does not establish popular sovereignty or universal suffrage.','chiso','\u5730\u79df','Land tax; the Meiji reform made monetary payment central to the new fiscal order.', ['meijiconst','oath'])
m(p,'The nation needs your time','Daily life','A household must balance schooling, work, taxes, and new obligations.',
'National education policy and military conscription expanded the state\'s reach into everyday life. School attendance developed unevenly and imposed real costs on households that depended on children\'s work. A government announcement did not produce immediate universal compliance.||Industrialization drew heavily on textile production and the labor of women and girls. Tomioka was a prominent model silk mill linked with imported machinery and expertise. Conditions varied across workplaces; one model institution should not stand for every factory.||Wage work could bring income, skills, and new experiences alongside discipline, long hours, and vulnerability. Avoid portraying industrial workers as either universally liberated or without agency. Their options were shaped by household needs, contracts, employers, and wider markets.',
'Why should one model silk mill not represent all industrial labor?', ['Conditions and workers\' experiences differed across institutions and industries.','Industrialization required no household decisions.','Only men participated in the textile economy.'],'An exemplary site can illuminate a process while remaining unrepresentative of many other workplaces.','jogaku','\u5973\u5b66','Women\'s education; its expansion and purposes were contested in modernizing society.', ['silk','iwakura'])
m(p,'Whose modernization?','Society','A national map looks unified, but communities remember different histories.',
'The Meiji state incorporated the Ryukyu Kingdom as Okinawa Prefecture in 1879. State-building also transformed Hokkaido and Ainu lives through settlement and assimilation policies. Modern national unity was made through unequal relationships, not simply inherited from antiquity.||Colonial rule in Taiwan and Korea imposed political domination and changing programs of economic and cultural control. People resisted, adapted, negotiated, and built their own movements. They should appear as historical actors rather than scenery in Japan\'s rise as a power.||Older status disabilities were formally altered, yet discrimination did not disappear automatically. The gap between legal reform and lived equality is a recurring historical theme. Evaluate modernization by asking who gained which rights, resources, and choices, and at whose expense.',
'What question makes a narrative of modernization more complete?', ['Who gained opportunities, and who faced dispossession, exclusion, or coercion?','Which technology proves everyone\'s life improved equally?','How can colonial subjects be removed from the account?'],'National success measured from the center can conceal very different experiences at its margins.','teikoku','\u5e1d\u56fd','Empire; a political order that can incorporate populations under unequal forms of rule.', ['ryukyu','ainu','meijiconst'])
m(p,'Making a national language','Language','A classroom privileges one form of Japanese over a child\'s home speech.',
'National education, print, administration, and later broadcasting helped spread standardized Japanese. Standardization increased communication across regions but also assigned prestige unevenly. A home language or dialect could become something a pupil was pressured to suppress.||Kokugo, national language, linked language to the modern state. The category was not culturally neutral in an expanding empire. Schooling and assimilation affected Ainu, Ryukyuan, and colonized communities in different ways that require attention to local history.||Written Japanese also changed as authors sought styles closer to speech. The process was uneven across genres. Modern standard Japanese is therefore a historical outcome, not the unaltered language spoken identically throughout the archipelago from its beginning.',
'Why is a standard language both enabling and political?', ['It eases some communication while privileging particular forms and identities.','It proves every earlier dialect was linguistically defective.','It emerges without institutions, teaching, or power.'],'A useful common standard can coexist with unequal treatment of other languages and dialects.','kokugo','\u56fd\u8a9e','National language; a modern institutional category linking language and state.', ['chj','ainu','ryukyu'])

p=era('taisho','Taisho','\u5927\u6b63','1912-1926','More voices. Unequal rights.','Party cabinets, mass media, household protest, and new identities test the limits of participation.','Cities, villages, and the imperial sphere','Newspaper front page',['suffrage','rice','chj'],'Party politics expanded within an imperial constitutional system, alongside repression of dissent.','Inflation, wage work, consumer culture, and rural hardship produced unequal versions of modern life.','Newspapers, magazines, fiction, and public speech enlarged debate without giving everyone equal access.')
m(p,'Democracy with an asterisk','World','A newspaper celebrates political participation while another story records repression.',
'Taisho democracy is a label for developments extending beyond the reign dates themselves. Party politics, civic movements, labor activism, and public debate grew in importance. The imperial constitutional structure nevertheless remained, and powerful actors stood outside electoral accountability.||Japan\'s role in the First World War brought economic and diplomatic opportunities while expanding imperial ambitions. Domestic liberalization did not automatically imply equal treatment of colonized peoples. Democracy at the center and coercion elsewhere could coexist.||Historical labels are useful invitations, not verdicts. Ask which groups participated, which institutions changed, and whose rights remained limited. The same period can contain meaningful democratic gains and serious constraints without one cancelling the other.',
'What does "Taisho democracy" require you to investigate?', ['The scope of participation and its limits, including the imperial context.','An assumption that all adults immediately gained identical rights.','A claim that repression entirely disappeared.'],'A period label should guide questions rather than substitute for institutional analysis.','minponshugi','\u6c11\u672c\u4e3b\u7fa9','People-centered government; associated with debates over popular interests within the imperial order.')
m(p,'The vote expands, the boundary remains','Power','The 1925 suffrage reform reaches the Diet.',
'The 1925 reform removed the property-tax qualification for voting by men aged twenty-five and over, while other exclusions remained. Women did not gain national voting rights through this law. Calling it universal suffrage without qualification obscures a major boundary.||The Peace Preservation Law was enacted in the same year, targeting challenges to the national polity and private property. Expanded electoral participation and stronger tools for political repression were not mutually exclusive developments. They advanced within the same system.||This pairing is a useful test of institutional reasoning. Counting voters tells you something important, but not everything, about democracy. Freedom to organize, advocate, criticize, and contest the rules is another dimension of political participation.',
'What best captures the political tension of 1925?', ['A wider male electorate accompanied a major new repressive law.','Women and men both gained unrestricted national suffrage.','Property qualifications expanded while political policing ended.'],'Participation has multiple dimensions; the franchise and freedom of political activity can move in different directions.','futsu senkyo','\u666e\u901a\u9078\u6319','Universal suffrage; in the 1925 context, a qualified expansion of male suffrage.', ['suffrage'])
m(p,'The price of dinner becomes political','Daily life','Rice prices rise faster than a household can manage.',
'The Rice Riots of 1918 emerged from anger over prices and spread across varied communities. Women were prominent in early protests associated with coastal Toyama. Household provisioning became a route into collective political action.||Wartime economic expansion did not benefit everyone equally. Inflation could erode purchasing power even while firms and some workers gained opportunities. A national growth story needs to be checked against household budgets and local employment conditions.||Urban consumer culture, cafes, and new entertainment coexisted with rural hardship and insecure work. The modern city offered new possibilities but was not the experience of the whole population. Social history asks how these different worlds were linked through markets and migration.',
'Why are food-price protests central to political history?', ['Household survival can mobilize people and challenge authorities.','Only formal officeholders can act politically.','Economic growth always protects every household\'s purchasing power.'],'Politics includes collective claims about everyday resources, not only elections and cabinets.','kome sodo','\u7c73\u9a12\u52d5','Rice disturbances or Rice Riots; especially the widespread protests of 1918.', ['rice'])
m(p,'A new woman, not one woman','Society','A magazine debates education, employment, marriage, and independence.',
'Women\'s organizations and writers challenged constraints on education, work, and public participation. The "new woman" and later "modern girl" were influential and contested cultural figures. Media images could exaggerate novelty or turn social debate into a stereotype.||Women\'s experiences differed by class, locality, family, and occupation. A fashionable urban image did not represent a farm worker or factory employee. Nor did the visibility of a few writers erase legal and political exclusions.||The 1923 Great Kanto Earthquake caused catastrophic destruction. In its aftermath, false rumors fueled massacres of Koreans and violence against others. This history requires recognizing how prejudice and misinformation can become collective violence, not treating disaster as purely natural.',
'Why should a magazine\'s "modern woman" not stand for all women?', ['It is a selective cultural image shaped by class, audience, and debate.','Women never participated in public arguments.','All women experienced identical work, family, and legal conditions.'],'Representation can reveal anxieties and aspirations without functioning as a social census.','atarashii onna','\u65b0\u3057\u3044\u5973','New woman; a contested figure in debates about women\'s roles and independence.', ['rice','suffrage','chj'])
m(p,'Writing starts sounding different','Language','A novel\'s prose feels closer to speech than an older formal document.',
'Genbun itchi names movements toward bringing written and spoken language closer together. It developed across the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, rather than beginning neatly with the Taisho reign. Writers experimented with voice, narration, and dialogue.||Mass-circulation newspapers and magazines made style a question of audience and accessibility. Different genres retained different conventions. A legal document, novel, advertisement, and everyday conversation did not suddenly converge into one uniform register.||Language change can create new forms of intimacy and authority. A narrator who sounds conversational may feel close to readers, but that voice is still crafted. Learn to distinguish the effect of naturalness from a literal transcript of how everyone spoke.',
'What does genbun itchi describe?', ['Efforts to bring written expression closer to spoken language.','A law requiring every genre to use identical sentences.','The abolition of all regional spoken varieties.'],'A stylistic movement can transform writing without erasing register or regional difference.','genbun itchi','\u8a00\u6587\u4e00\u81f4','The movement toward greater alignment of written and spoken language.', ['chj'])

p=era('war','Empire & war','\u662d\u548c','1926-1945','Read this chapter with care','Trace authoritarian power, imperial aggression, mobilization, civilian suffering, and defeat.','Japan and the Asia-Pacific war zones','Civilian diary',['manchuria','kono','constitution'],'Military institutions, political violence, imperial authority, and mobilization narrowed space for dissent.','Rationing, conscription, forced labor, displacement, and bombing transformed lives across the empire and beyond.','Propaganda, censorship, official euphemisms, private writing, and survivor testimony tell different kinds of truth.')
p['sensitive']=True
m(p,'An empire at war before 1941','World','A timeline starts at Pearl Harbor. You need to repair what it leaves out.',
'Japanese officers staged the railway explosion used to justify the occupation of Manchuria in 1931. Manchukuo was established as a puppet state in 1932. Full-scale war with China expanded in 1937, years before the attack on Pearl Harbor in December 1941.||Japanese forces committed extensive atrocities, including mass killing and sexual violence at Nanjing. Imperial rule also involved forced labor and the coercive military "comfort women" system. These are central to the history, not optional footnotes to military campaigns.||Beginning the story only with Japan\'s conflict with the United States obscures years of war and suffering in Asia. A complete chronology must include Chinese, Korean, Taiwanese, Southeast Asian, and other experiences without treating any population as a single uniform actor.',
'Why is 1941 an inadequate starting point for the wider war history?', ['Japanese expansion and large-scale war in China preceded Pearl Harbor.','The conflict began only after the atomic bombings.','Manchukuo was unrelated to Japanese military expansion.'],'Different national narratives choose different beginnings. A connected chronology must include the earlier Asian war.','Manshu','\u6e80\u5dde','Manchuria, a region in northeast China central to Japanese imperial expansion.')
m(p,'Who can stop escalation?','Power','A cabinet announces restraint, but military actors continue expanding.',
'The 1931 crisis exposed the difficulty of controlling military action. The cabinet adopted a nonexpansion policy, yet the Kwantung Army continued operations. Institutional arrangements, political calculations, and subsequent acceptance helped turn insubordination into enlarged commitments.||Political assassinations, repression, and military influence weakened party government. This was not a simple process in which one identical view instantly replaced all disagreement. The erosion of constraints involved multiple actors, decisions, and failures of accountability.||Total mobilization drew labor, industry, information, and household life into the war effort. Analyze who could issue demands, who implemented them, and who bore the consequences. Explaining these mechanisms does not excuse the choices or remove responsibility.',
'What does the Manchurian crisis reveal about political control?', ['A formal policy can fail when institutions cannot or will not restrain armed actors.','A cabinet statement automatically determines every military action.','Escalation occurs without decisions or responsibility.'],'Authority on paper must be connected with the capacity and willingness to enforce limits.','sodoin','\u7dcf\u52d5\u54e1','Total mobilization: organizing society\'s people and resources for war.', ['manchuria'])
m(p,'War reaches the kitchen','Daily life','A household must find food while meeting neighborhood obligations.',
'Wartime life involved rationing, shortages, conscription, labor mobilization, and increasingly severe air attacks. Experiences changed over time and differed by location and status. The home front was neither fully separate from war nor experienced identically by all civilians.||Tonarigumi, neighborhood associations, helped organize distribution, civil defense, information, and surveillance. The same local relationship could provide practical support and impose pressure to conform. Mutual aid and state control can operate through the same institution.||Colonized and occupied populations faced distinct forms of coercion, displacement, and deprivation. Keep these differences visible when comparing civilian experiences. Shared suffering does not erase unequal power, aggression, or responsibility.',
'Why can a neighborhood association be both helpful and coercive?', ['It can distribute support while enforcing surveillance and conformity.','Practical assistance makes political pressure impossible.','Only national ministries can exercise social control.'],'Power can work through everyday relationships as well as formal state offices.','tonarigumi','\u96a3\u7d44','Wartime neighborhood association involved in mobilization, distribution, and local control.')
m(p,'Defeat is not the end of consequences','Society','A survivor\'s account continues long after a surrender date.',
'The Battle of Okinawa, firebombing of cities, and the atomic bombings of Hiroshima on August 6 and Nagasaki on August 9, 1945, caused devastating civilian suffering. The Soviet Union entered the war against Japan in August. Japan announced surrender on August 15; formal signing followed on September 2.||The relative weight of the atomic bombings, Soviet entry, and internal political decisions in producing surrender is debated. Recognizing that debate does not require uncertainty about the occurrence or human consequences of the bombings and invasion.||Hibakusha, atomic-bomb survivors, faced illness, loss, discrimination, and the work of testimony and advocacy. Other survivors across Asia had their own long aftermaths. A historical ending date does not mark the end of trauma, displacement, or contested memory.',
'Why distinguish a war\'s formal end from its social aftermath?', ['Survivors\' health, displacement, discrimination, and memory continued beyond surrender.','Every consequence ended when a document was signed.','The existence of debate about surrender means no events can be established.'],'Chronological closure in a timeline is not closure in people\'s lives.','hibakusha','\u88ab\u7206\u8005','People affected by the atomic bombings, commonly referring to survivors.')
m(p,'Read the words that hide the action','Language','An official phrase makes coercion sound ordinary.',
'Kokutai, often translated as national polity, carried powerful ideological meanings around the imperial state. Official language could frame obedience as moral duty and dissent as a threat to the nation. Definitions were political, not merely descriptive.||Euphemisms matter. The label "comfort women" must be explained as referring to a coercive military sexual system, not accepted as a neutral description of voluntary work. Source language can conceal violence even while providing evidence of the institutions that organized it.||Compare official statements with private diaries, records, and survivor testimony. Each has a context and limitations; they need not be treated as equally complete or interchangeable. Historical reading asks both what a source says and what its wording makes difficult to see.',
'What should you do when a source uses a euphemism for coercion?', ['Explain the term\'s actual historical referent and compare evidence.','Repeat it without context as if it were a neutral description.','Assume official language cannot obscure violence.'],'Source criticism includes identifying how vocabulary can normalize or hide relations of domination.','kokutai','\u56fd\u4f53','National polity; a politically charged concept associated with the imperial order.', ['kono','suffrage'])
