p=era('postwar','Rebuilding & growth','\u662d\u548c','1945-1989','Who gets a new beginning?','Reconstruction, constitutional change, mass consumption, and the unequal costs of rapid growth.','Tokyo, industrial regions, and Okinawa','Radio',['constitution','article9','minamata','okinawa1972'],'Popular sovereignty and a parliamentary cabinet replaced the imperial constitutional order. Occupation, the US alliance, and domestic coalitions shaped the new system.','Factories, urban migration, appliances, and education transformed households, but job security and prosperity were uneven.','Modern written reforms and changing institutions reshaped public language; politeness remained sensitive to context.')
m(p,'Rebuild the rules, not just the roads','World','In a damaged neighborhood, housing and the rules of government both need rebuilding.',
'After surrender in 1945, Japan was occupied under Allied authority led by the United States. The occupation brought demilitarization and major political, social, and economic reforms. Japanese officials, political groups, and citizens also influenced implementation; society was not simply an empty page.||The constitution came into force in 1947. It located sovereignty in the people, protected rights, and made the emperor a symbol rather than the source of sovereign power. Compare this with the Meiji constitution: the change concerns the basis of authority, not just the name of an office.||The occupation of mainland Japan ended in 1952, while Okinawa remained under US administration until 1972. A single national timeline can hide different regional experiences. Cold War priorities also altered the occupation agenda, strengthening some conservative institutions after earlier reforms.',
'Why is 1952 an incomplete endpoint for occupation history?', ['Okinawa remained under US administration until 1972.','The 1947 constitution had not yet been written.','All local experiences matched the mainland timeline.'],'Regional timelines reveal how sovereignty and everyday experience can change at different moments.','sengo','\u6226\u5f8c','Postwar; a period label whose social meaning extends beyond a single ending date.')
m(p,'Where sovereignty lives now','Power','You compare two constitutional diagrams, one from 1889 and one from 1947.',
'Under the postwar constitution, the Diet is the highest organ of state power and the sole law-making organ. It designates the prime minister. The cabinet exercises executive power and is collectively responsible to the Diet; the emperor does not independently choose government policy.||Women gained the national franchise during the occupation and voted in the 1946 general election. Constitutional equality opened new possibilities without automatically removing discrimination, unequal care burdens, or barriers to representation. Rights on paper require institutions and action to become lived realities.||Article 9 renounces war and the threat or use of force as means of settling international disputes. The creation of the Self-Defense Forces in 1954, the US alliance, and successive interpretations generated continuing political and legal debates. Distinguish the constitutional text from later policy and interpretation.',
'Which statement best describes postwar constitutional authority?', ['Sovereignty rests with the people, and the cabinet is responsible to the Diet.','The emperor personally appoints a government without Diet designation.','The prime minister rules independently of representative institutions.'],'The emperor formally appoints the prime minister as designated by the Diet, rather than exercising a personal political choice.','kokumin shuken','\u56fd\u6c11\u4e3b\u6a29','Popular sovereignty: political authority is grounded in the people.', ['constitution','article9'])
m(p,'The refrigerator has a backstory','Daily life','A family buys an appliance that changes the work done at home.',
'Rapid economic growth transformed production, housing, transport, and consumption. Rural migrants supplied urban labor, and expanding education changed expectations. The 1964 Tokyo Olympics and the new Tokaido Shinkansen became powerful images of reconstruction and technological confidence.||A salaried husband and full-time homemaker became an influential household ideal. It never described everyone. Farmers, shopkeepers, women wage workers, small-business employees, and workers without secure company positions experienced growth differently. Household work remained essential to the paid economy.||An appliance is therefore evidence of more than engineering. Its purchase depends on income, electricity, housing, credit, and decisions about labor. Ask who could afford it and whether it reduced work, raised standards of domestic care, or did both.',
'What is missing from a story that equates postwar life with secure male corporate employment?', ['The many households and workers outside that influential ideal.','The existence of any consumer goods.','The possibility that urban migration affected families.'],'An influential social model is not a census of everyone\'s experience.','kaden','\u5bb6\u96fb','Household electrical appliances; objects that connect consumption with domestic labor.')
m(p,'Growth has a downstream cost','Society','A fishing community suffers while a factory appears to prosper.',
'Minamata disease exposed the human costs of industrial pollution. Methylmercury released by factories entered food chains, and people were poisoned through contaminated fish and shellfish. The disease was officially reported in Minamata in 1956; recognition of causes and responsibility involved long delays and struggles.||Patients, families, scientists, journalists, lawyers, and campaigners challenged corporate and governmental responses. Economic growth did not automatically protect health. The question is who received benefits, who bore risks, and who had the power to make harm visible.||Other conflicts also complicated images of consensus: labor struggles, protests over the US-Japan security treaty, and disputes over military bases in Okinawa. A society can achieve rising aggregate income while remaining divided over distribution, security, and justice.',
'What question best exposes the politics of industrial pollution?', ['Who benefits, who is harmed, and who can force institutions to respond?','Did the national economy grow, regardless of local harm?','Can prosperity be measured only by the number of factories?'],'Aggregate growth can conceal concentrated harms and unequal access to remedies.','kogai','\u516c\u5bb3','Pollution-related public harm, especially associated with industrial environmental damage.', ['minamata','okinawa1972'])
m(p,'Polite does not mean timeless','Language','A colleague changes wording between a customer call and lunch with a friend.',
'Contemporary Japanese has several resources for managing relationships. Teineigo uses polite forms, such as desu and masu. Sonkeigo raises the person being discussed; kenjogo presents one\'s own side humbly in relation to another. These are grammatical and social choices, not measures of personal worth.||The appropriate form depends on setting, role, relationship, and what the speaker is doing. An employee representing a company may refer humbly to a colleague when speaking to an outside customer, even if that colleague is senior inside the company. Inside and outside affiliations matter alongside rank.||Do not turn these observations into a rule that all Japanese people always act alike. Usage varies, conventions change, and mistakes are normal in learning. TOKI audio models contemporary pronunciation; it does not reconstruct a Heian conversation or a wartime broadcast.',
'Why might an employee use a humble form for a senior colleague when speaking to a customer?', ['The colleague belongs to the speaker\'s own side in that interaction.','The colleague has permanently lost all workplace authority.','Japanese politeness depends only on age.'],'Relational positioning changes with the audience and the activity.','keigo','\u656c\u8a9e','Honorific and polite language resources used to manage social relationships.', ['chj'])
p=era('heisei','Heisei','\u5e73\u6210','1989-2019','Beyond the boom','Follow the bubble\'s aftermath, changing work, major disasters, and new kinds of belonging.','Metropolitan centers and regional communities','Mobile phone',['bubble','quake','reconstruction','ainu'],'Parliamentary government continued while party competition and economic policy changed. Institutions mattered more than imperial era labels.','Asset losses, insecure work, changing families, and digital connection produced different experiences across generations.','Digital registers and global borrowing expanded expressive options; minority-language revitalization challenged narratives of uniformity.')
m(p,'When the bubble is no longer a bubble','World','A business owes money against property now worth much less.',
'Stock and land prices rose sharply in the late 1980s and fell after the bubble burst. Falling collateral values and financial-sector problems affected borrowing, investment, and confidence. An asset-price decline can reshape the real economy through balance sheets, not just through disappointing headlines.||The phrase lost decades summarizes prolonged economic difficulty but can flatten a complicated period. Industries, regions, firms, and households did not all move together. New technologies, cultural production, and international connections continued even amid weak growth and deflationary pressures.||Separate a historical explanation from an inevitable destiny. Policy choices, financial institutions, global changes, and demographics interacted. Neither a single cultural trait nor a new emperor\'s era name can explain all economic outcomes.',
'How can a fall in property prices affect a company beyond its property portfolio?', ['Weaker collateral and balance sheets can restrict borrowing and investment.','It changes only the spelling of the company\'s accounts.','It guarantees identical outcomes for every worker and firm.'],'Asset values can influence credit and business decisions through institutional channels.','baburu','\u30d0\u30d6\u30eb','Bubble; a loanword commonly used for the late-1980s asset boom and its aftermath.', ['bubble'])
m(p,'An era name is not a constitution','Power','A newspaper changes its date heading. Has the system of government changed too?',
'Heisei began in 1989 with a new emperor. Unlike the Meiji Restoration, this change did not replace the postwar constitutional framework. An imperial era name is a way of dating and periodizing events, not a guarantee of a new political system.||Party competition, electoral arrangements, bureaucracy, business, organized groups, and public opinion all affected policy. Calling an entire period conservative, reformist, or stagnant can hide disagreements within institutions and changes across time. Identify the particular decision and the actors involved.||A useful comparison keeps levels separate: the symbolic monarchy, the elected Diet, the cabinet, local government, and economic institutions are not interchangeable. Ask who actually has authority over the policy you are trying to explain.',
'What follows automatically from a new imperial era name under the postwar constitution?', ['A change in official era dating, not replacement of the constitutional system.','A return to the emperor\'s personal control of policy.','The abolition of representative government.'],'Period labels organize time; institutional changes require their own evidence.','gengo','\u5143\u53f7','An era name used in Japanese date notation.', ['constitution'])
m(p,'Two jobs, two kinds of security','Daily life','Two friends work in the same building but have different contracts.',
'Employment arrangements shape income, benefits, training, and the ability to plan. Nonregular work includes several different categories, such as part-time, fixed-term, and dispatched work. A label can hide differences in hours, choice, protections, and household circumstances.||The long-term corporate employment ideal was never universal. Economic restructuring made its limits more visible, especially to some younger entrants and women workers. Family formation and care decisions are connected with wages, working time, housing, and access to support.||Avoid treating every difficult life course as an individual failure or every worker as equally constrained. Historical explanation connects personal decisions with available options. To compare two households, ask about security and unpaid labor as well as annual income.',
'Why can equal current wages conceal unequal job security?', ['Contract duration, benefits, and future opportunities may differ.','Everyone in one building necessarily has the same employer and contract.','Household care work is unrelated to employment options.'],'Income is one dimension of an employment relationship, not its complete structure.','hiseiki','\u975e\u6b63\u898f','Nonregular employment; an umbrella label for diverse work arrangements.')
m(p,'Three disasters, many aftermaths','Society','A map shows a disaster zone; a family\'s recovery does not fit inside it.',
'The 1995 Kobe earthquake and the March 11, 2011, Great East Japan Earthquake transformed public discussion of disaster preparation and mutual aid. In 2011, the earthquake triggered a devastating tsunami and the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear accident. These are linked events with distinct mechanisms and consequences.||Recovery includes housing, livelihoods, schools, health, social networks, and decisions about return. Infrastructure reconstruction is not identical to restoring a community. People may reasonably make different decisions because their ages, work, family ties, and risks differ.||Volunteer activity and local organizations became important parts of recovery. They do not replace public responsibility. Listen to affected communities rather than assuming resilience means people can recover without resources or that every resident wants the same future.',
'Which measure captures recovery beyond rebuilding physical infrastructure?', ['Whether people regain viable livelihoods, social ties, and choices about where to live.','The number of repaired roads alone.','A single announcement that all residents feel recovered.'],'Physical reconstruction and social recovery are connected but distinct.','fukko','\u5fa9\u8208','Recovery or reconstruction after devastation, including social and economic rebuilding.', ['quake','reconstruction'])
m(p,'One archipelago, more than one voice','Language','An online conversation and a language-revival workshop both challenge your textbook.',
'Digital communication offers new combinations of scripts, abbreviations, emoji, and conversational styles. Katakana commonly marks loanwords and other expressive functions, but not every katakana word has an English origin or the same meaning as its source. Context matters more than visual resemblance.||Japanese is not the only language connected with the archipelago. Ainu is distinct from Japanese. Ryukyuan languages belong to the Japonic family but are not simply interchangeable varieties of standard Japanese. Political labels and linguistic relationships need to be distinguished.||Revitalization concerns communities, education, cultural practice, and intergenerational transmission. Learning about a language as endangered is not the same as treating its speakers as relics. Contemporary people actively decide how to preserve, adapt, and use their linguistic inheritance.',
'Why is it misleading to assume all languages of the archipelago are standard Japanese dialects?', ['There are distinct languages and histories, including Ainu and Ryukyuan languages.','A script always proves that two languages are identical.','Modern national borders establish every linguistic relationship.'],'Political territory does not determine language classification or cultural identity.','tayosei','\u591a\u69d8\u6027','Diversity; here, the coexistence of different linguistic and social experiences.', ['ainu','chj'])
p=era('reiwa','Reiwa & today','\u4ee4\u548c','2019-2026','History is still happening','Read today through institutions, demographic change, diverse communities, and the discipline of checking dates.','Across Japan, connected globally','Community notebook',['pm2026','censusfinal','constitution','ainu'],'The postwar constitutional framework continues. Distinguish dated political events from claims about an automatically updating present.','Population decline and aging affect care, work, transport, and local services in uneven ways.','Contemporary communication changes across relationships, generations, regions, and multilingual settings.')
m(p,'The present needs a timestamp','World','Two pages say latest. One was published a year before the other.',
'Reiwa began in 2019. This chapter is a researched snapshot dated October 10, 2026, not a live news service. Recent history is unusually vulnerable to stale numbers, changing officeholders, and accounts revised as more evidence becomes available.||Distinguish the date an event occurred from the date a report was published. A census released in 2026 can describe a population counted in 2025. A revised result may supersede an earlier preliminary count without meaning that millions of people moved overnight.||Bring the same source criticism used for chronicles to digital information: who produced this, when, using what method, and with what incentive? A polished chart or confident sentence is not a substitute for those questions. Today becomes history through records that must still be interpreted.',
'What dates should you check when using a recently released statistic?', ['Both the reference date of the data and the publication or revision date.','Only the date you happen to read the page.','Only the year printed in the page\'s title.'],'Observation, publication, and revision are separate events.','shiryo','\u8cc7\u6599','Source or documentary material used to investigate a question.', ['census','censusfinal'])
m(p,'Follow the appointment chain','Power','A ceremony makes the news. You want to know where the decision was made.',
'On February 18, 2026, the National Diet designated Sanae Takaichi as prime minister, as recorded by the Prime Minister\'s Office. This is a dated event in the current chapter, not a claim that a static app can monitor every subsequent political development.||Under the constitution, the emperor appoints the prime minister as designated by the Diet. The cabinet is responsible to the Diet. Ceremony, legal procedure, and political choice are related but distinct parts of the process; the ceremony does not make the emperor an independent policymaker.||Compare this with earlier eras. Fujiwara marriage politics, warrior-vassal networks, Tokugawa domain control, and postwar parliamentary designation are different mechanisms of power. Similar titles or impressive rituals do not make their institutions equivalent.',
'In the postwar appointment chain, who makes the constitutional designation of the prime minister?', ['The National Diet.','The emperor acting on personal political preference.','A hereditary council of daimyo.'],'Article 67 provides for designation by the Diet; Article 6 describes appointment by the emperor.','shusho','\u9996\u76f8','Prime minister; a common term for the head of the cabinet.', ['pm2026','constitution'])
m(p,'What a smaller population changes','Daily life','A town must choose how to maintain transport and care with fewer residents.',
'The final 2025 census population count, published September 29, 2026, reported 122,972,528 residents nationwide, down from 126,146,099 in 2020. These are census reference populations, not an automatic count of people living in Japan at this instant.||Population decline and aging are not uniform across places. A growing neighborhood and a shrinking rural settlement can exist within the same national trend. Fewer school-age children, changing care needs, and the cost of maintaining infrastructure create different local tradeoffs.||A demographic statistic is a starting point for inquiry, not a verdict that a society has no future. Ask how migration, work arrangements, technology, public services, and community decisions affect people\'s options. Policies distribute both opportunities and burdens.',
'Why is a national population total insufficient for planning a local bus route?', ['Age structure, settlement patterns, and needs vary between communities.','Every locality changes at exactly the national rate.','Census counts describe opinions rather than residents.'],'Aggregate totals do not reveal the geography or daily needs behind them.','shoshikoreika','\u5c11\u5b50\u9ad8\u9f62\u5316','A declining birthrate and population aging, often discussed together in Japan.', ['censusfinal'])
m(p,'Belonging is not one biography','Society','A community project brings together people with different languages and family histories.',
'Contemporary Japan includes regional, Indigenous, migrant, religious, and other diverse communities. A single image of a homogeneous household cannot explain this range. People can share institutions while having different experiences of inclusion, discrimination, and opportunity.||Upopoy, the National Ainu Museum and Park, presents Ainu culture as living and supports transmission and revitalization. This changes the question from whether a culture belongs only in the past to how communities exercise agency in the present. Museums themselves can be studied as institutions of representation.||When considering coexistence, ask who helps decide the rules, which languages information appears in, and who can access services. Inclusion is more than the presence of different foods or festivals. It also involves voice, resources, recognition, and practical rights.',
'Which question goes beyond a purely decorative view of cultural diversity?', ['Can different communities shape decisions and access information and services?','How many festival costumes can be displayed?','Can everyone be described by the same household stereotype?'],'Recognition becomes more substantive when connected with agency and access.','tabunka kyosei','\u591a\u6587\u5316\u5171\u751f','Multicultural coexistence; a term used in discussions of living together across cultural differences.', ['ainu'])
m(p,'Choose a relationship, then a phrase','Language','You need help from a stranger, then report back to a close friend.',
'Sumimasen can attract attention, express an apology, or acknowledge an imposition, depending on context. One English equivalent does not capture every use. Arigato gozaimasu is a polite expression of thanks; a shorter arigato often suits a closer or less formal relationship.||A request such as mo ichido onegai shimasu means please say or do it once more in context. The thing repeated is often understood rather than fully restated. Learning an interaction includes identifying the speaker, audience, purpose, and shared situation, not just translating each word.||These examples are contemporary learning phrases, not authenticated dialogue from ancient Japan. The app\'s pronunciation uses your device\'s modern Japanese speech engine when available. Historical language study and everyday conversation are connected but distinct learning goals.',
'You did not hear a museum guide. Which contemporary request fits?', ['Mo ichido onegai shimasu: please say it once more.','A claim that prehistoric people used exactly the same phrase.','An unrelated historical title presented as a request.'],'A useful phrase solves a communicative problem within a relationship and setting.','sumimasen','\u3059\u307f\u307e\u305b\u3093','Excuse me / sorry; its function depends on the interaction.', ['chj'])
